<Base title="Sources & evidence"><Heading label="A transparent record" title="The work behind the words." description="This site draws on my 27-page brag sheet and its first-person reflections. The source is author-provided; it is not independent verification."/><div class="prose"><h2>Source and scope</h2><p>Primary source: “Teacher Rec Brag Sheet | Varshith College,” 27 pages, supplied for this website. Page references refer to PDF page numbers, including the opening profile. The original document remains private because it was prepared for recommendations and contains material beyond the scope of a public site.</p><p>Site content was adapted on November 11, 2026. The document’s reporting date is not established. Present-tense roles in the source should not be taken as independently confirmed current appointments.</p><h2 id="figures">How to read the figures</h2><p><strong>November 2026 Uplift update:</strong> Varshith supplied a summer 2026 case study reporting 3,500+ student learning hours, 850+ volunteer service hours, 200+ showcase attendees, and 70+ innovation ideas covering all 17 SDGs. Camp dates: July 18–August 8, 2026, as confirmed by Varshith. Camp host locations: MidPointe Library and Mason Public Library. The later inaugural NextGen Coders case study specifies January 6–March 17, 2026 at Mason Public Library, with 600+ student learning hours, 450+ volunteer service hours, 30 learners, and 60+ showcase attendees. These more specific figures supersede the earlier preliminary 500+/650+ cohort figures; the cohort is not counted as an additional program or added to summer totals. The detailed case study labels the 3,500+ figure as student learning hours, resolving the inconsistent “volunteer hours” wording in the brief. Supporting attendance logs and hour-calculation methodology have not been reviewed.</p><ul><li><strong>Approximately 70 camp students:</strong> pp. 4, 18, 21–22. Earlier participant estimate from the brag sheet; the later program update identifies summer 2026. This is distinct from the 70+ innovation ideas now reported.</li><li><strong>More than 200 showcase attendees:</strong> pp. 4, 21–22. Includes over 150 parents and family members. These are not additional students and are not added to a total “people served” figure.</li><li><strong>12 startup teams:</strong> pp. 3, 23. Initial Venture Studio program. Four advancing to competition and one qualifying nationally are self-reported; event names and dates require confirmation.</li><li><strong>Approximately 60 ATMIYA participants:</strong> pp. 7, 17, 26. The source also uses “more than 60”; the site retains the more conservative approximate figure. No program period supplied.</li></ul><p>Reach, activity, and intended benefit are different kinds of evidence. No claim of measured lasting educational or clinical outcomes is made.</p><h2>Claims requiring verification</h2><ul><li>Supporting programs and the specific showcase date for Innovation Camp, school-board presentations, and OSBA.</li><li>Junior Achievement Hall of Fame: selection is recorded on p. 12; speaking date, delivery, and selection denominator are unconfirmed.</li><li>Junior Achievement competition names, levels, years, and ranks. Precise rankings are withheld.</li><li>Service hours across overlapping activities and reporting periods. No aggregate is published.</li><li>Uplift’s legal status and the relationship between the broader organization and Ohio division. No tax-exempt status or donation eligibility is asserted.</li><li>FBLA award dates and official results; individual Model UN conference records.</li><li>Current role dates, project operating status, partnerships, and pilots.</li><li>Research manuscript title, venue, and publication status. “Approved, publication pending” is retained as a source-reported status.</li></ul><h2>What is not published</h2><p>Private academic records, home addresses, phone numbers, family information, recommendation material, participant names, and photographs not supplied for publication are excluded. The public contact email was explicitly provided for the site.</p><h2>Photographs and links</h2><p>The portrait and Uplift program photos were supplied by Varshith for this site. Published camp photos are converted for the web with embedded metadata removed; captions describe visible activities without identifying participants. Original abstract illustrations represent curiosity and the process of building; they do not depict actual events or measured results. No stock or generated photographs are used to depict real events. The reference screenshot guided the layout only. Project website addresses come from the supplied material; they are not evidence that every project claim has been verified.</p><h2>Keeping this record useful</h2><p>As supporting records become available, each entry can gain a dated source, a permission-cleared photograph, or an official result. Corrections should update both the public wording and the related source note.</p></div></Base>
